# bug-nilpy-immediately-invoked-lambda-is-not-callable
#
# TWO independent faults, and the second was hiding behind the first:
#
# 1. A parenthesised lambda used directly as a callee was never BOXED. The
#    lambda lowering yields a raw closure pointer; PyBoxCallableValue is what
#    turns one into the variant the dynamic-call path recognises, and it ran
#    only on an assignment right-hand side. So `f = lambda ...` then `f(...)`
#    worked and the inline form raised "object is not callable".
#
# 2. `(lambda a, b: ...)` was parsed as a TUPLE. PyTupleParenAhead saw the comma
#    in the lambda's PARAMETER LIST and read it as a tuple separator, so a
#    multi-parameter lambda became a TPyList. A zero- or one-parameter lambda
#    has no such comma, which is why the bug presented as callability rather
#    than as parsing.
#
# The tuple rows below are the gate on fault 2 not being over-corrected:
# `(lambda v: v + 1, 5)` IS a 2-tuple in CPython — a lambda body stops at a
# comma inside a display, so only the commas BEFORE the colon are parameters.
#
# Pinned does not even parse line 1. Diffed against CPython (tools/pydiff.py).

print((lambda a, b: a - b)(9, 4))
print((lambda: 7)())
print((lambda x: x * 3)(5))

f = lambda a, b: a - b
print(f(9, 4))
g = lambda: 7
print(g())
print(list(map(lambda v: v + 1, [1, 2])))
handlers = {"a": lambda v: v + 100}
print(handlers["a"](2))
lst = [lambda v: v * 10]
print(lst[0](3))

# a real TUPLE must still be a tuple, and a lambda INSIDE one must still work
t = (1, 2, 3)
print(t)
pair = (lambda v: v + 1, 5)
print(pair[0](pair[1]))
print(())
print((7,))
print(sorted([3, 1, 2], key=lambda v: -v))
print((lambda a, b, c: a + b + c)(1, 2, 3))
